meta {
  name: Library, assets and curriculum
  seq: 10
}

docs {
  The library is content: books, notes, documents and past papers, each with an
  audience that decides who may read it. The curriculum is the syllabus those
  things are taught from. Folder 11 maps one onto the other.

  Two preconditions this folder cannot create for itself.

  The caller needs a person row at this school. owner_person_id on a content
  item is taken from the token, never from the body, so POST /library answers
  400 until a person exists whose cas_user_id is the token's subject. Request 1
  creates it; scripts/e2e.sh does the same thing for the same reason.

  The school needs S3 credentials, stored with
  PUT /api/v1/schools/:id/profiles/s3 - request 02/06 does that. docker compose
  now runs MinIO as `object-store` and creates the bucket, so this works on a
  stock checkout; it did not until 2026-09-22, and request 02/06 additionally
  sent a field name the API refuses, so this folder had never once run green.

  Without a store, every request that signs or inspects an object - 2, 5, 6 and
  7 - answers 503 "this school has no object store configured", and the reads
  that depend on the asset they would have created fail with them. That is the
  honest failure: an asset is a promise about bytes, and with nowhere for the
  bytes to go, no row is written either.
}
